{
  "teamId": 145,
  "year": 2022,
  "narrative": "The 2022 Chicago White Sox entered the season as legitimate American League contenders, armed with a talented roster and high expectations after back-to-back division titles. But the opener in Detroit set an ominous tone: Liam Hendriks, the team's elite closer, was tagged for multiple crushing blows in the same game, a preview of the bullpen volatility that would haunt Chicago all year. April ended at 8-12, the team already treading water before May had even arrived.\n\nMay and June brought a split personality. The offense showed flashes — Luis Robert's towering home run, José Abreu coming through in the clutch, Jake Burger announcing himself with a go-ahead blast — but for every surge, a gut-punch followed. Josh Naylor single-handedly dismantled the bullpen in Cleveland, and the roster's injury concerns mounted. Still, the White Sox steadied themselves enough to post a 15-12 May and hung around the fringes of contention heading into summer.\n\nJuly ignited something. A 16-11 record was the team's best month of the season, fueled by timely hitting from Andrew Vaughn, Luis Robert, Leury García, and AJ Pollock delivering a go-ahead single that embodied the scrappy, opportunistic baseball the South Siders could play at their best. For a few weeks, it looked like the pieces might finally click into place and a playoff push was truly on.\n\nBut August and the stretch run told a different story. The team stumbled to 13-16 in August, with Hendriks once again the central figure in late heartbreaks. September offered a modest rally — Romy Gonzalez, Elvis Andrus, and José Abreu providing moments of life — but it was all too little, too late. The White Sox finished exactly 81-81, a perfectly mediocre record that masked both the promise and the dysfunction of a franchise that spent 2022 spinning its wheels, ending the year on the outside of the playoffs looking in.",
